+We first investigate how an existing knowledge graph can supply information missing from a model's input. In column type annotation, a column of people accompanied by birth and death dates may lack the information needed to identify their common occupation. Linking the names to a knowledge graph can supply that information, but the graph may assign several valid types to each person. These types may also differ from the annotation needs. To address these issues, we propose KGLink to select relevant graph descriptions and integrate them with a pre-trained table encoder. Through supervised fine-tuning, the encoder learns their correspondence to the target labels~\cite{kglink-publication}.
 
-KGLink's use of external knowledge depends on the coverage of the existing graph and the labeled data available for fine-tuning. When a suitable graph is unavailable or new labels arrive with few examples, the system needs another way to obtain and use knowledge. We explore using a large language model's semantic capabilities with a graph constructed from task data. We propose GORAG for dynamic few-shot text classification. GORAG constructs weighted keyword--label relations, retrieves candidate labels by connecting query keywords, and updates the graph as new labels and texts arrive~\cite{gorag-paper}. The system incorporates new information through index updates without requiring a predefined knowledge graph or model fine-tuning.
+KGLink's use of external knowledge depends on the coverage of the existing graph and the labeled data available for fine-tuning. When a suitable graph is unavailable or new labels arrive with few examples, the system needs another way to obtain and use knowledge \cite{}. Hence, we explore using a large language model's semantic capabilities with a graph constructed from task data. As a result, we propose GORAG for dynamic few-shot text classification task. GORAG constructs weighted keyword-label relations, retrieves candidate labels by connecting query keywords, and updates the graph as new labels and texts arrive~\cite{gorag-paper}. The system incorporates new information through index updates without requiring a predefined knowledge graph or model fine-tuning.
 
-Building a graph from task data extends structural augmentation to settings without a predefined knowledge graph. For question answering and generation, however, retrieving candidate labels is no longer sufficient. The model needs facts that jointly support an answer, and a summary can require several complementary information items. Individually relevant passages can repeat facts or leave their connections implicit, while a short path between query anchors can omit required details. We propose AGRAG to construct a graph from the text corpus and select a connected evidence subgraph using query-dependent node influence and semantic edge costs. Subgraph expansion and hybrid passage retrieval supply additional facts and context for answer generation~\cite{agrag-paper}.
+Building a graph from task data extends structural augmentation to settings without a predefined knowledge graph. However, for question answering and generation, retrieving candidate labels is no longer sufficient. The model needs facts that jointly support an answer, and a summary can require several complementary information items \cite{}. Individually relevant passages can repeat facts or leave their connections implicit, while a short path between query anchors can omit required details. To address these issues, we propose AGRAG to construct a graph from the text corpus and select a connected evidence subgraph using query-dependent node influence and semantic edge costs. Subgraph expansion and hybrid passage retrieval supply additional facts and context for answer generation~\cite{agrag-paper}.
 
-Multimodal question answering introduces a further requirement for task-related graph construction and retrieval. Relevant facts can be distributed across text and images, and the visual details needed from an image depend on the question. A relation stated directly in that question may also correspond to several relations in the evidence. We propose MGRAG to build query-specific evidence graphs and match query relations to semantic paths~\cite{mgrag-paper}. Query-aware construction selects relevant facts, while path matching checks their correspondence to the requested relationships. The selected structure also guides the recomputation of cached model states when the evidence is processed for answering.
+On the other hand, multimodal question answering introduces a further requirement for task-related graph construction and retrieval \cite{}. Relevant facts can be distributed across text and images, and the visual details needed from an image depend on the question \cite{}. 
+In this scenario, different queries may need different visual evidences given the same image, and a relation stated directly in that query may also correspond to several relations in the evidence. 
+To address these issues, we propose MGRAG to build query-specific evidence graphs and match query relations to semantic paths~\cite{mgrag-paper}. Query-aware construction selects relevant facts, while path matching checks their correspondence to the requested relationships. The selected structure also guides the reuse of the LLM's KV cache when the evidence is processed for answering.
 
 In this thesis, we study how explicit knowledge structures can help language models obtain and use the evidence required by a task. We begin with knowledge-enriched representation learning, then reduce the dependence on existing knowledge graphs and task-specific fine-tuning through graph-based retrieval. We further investigate connected textual evidence and query-aware multimodal matching as two extensions to more complex answer requirements.
 
